# ============================================================
# Call Analyzer — .gitignore
#
# Two rules govern this file, and breaking either one is how .gitignore
# files go wrong:
#
#   1. Order matters. A later pattern overrides an earlier one, so every
#      `!negation` must come *after* the rule it is carving an exception
#      out of. The "tracked by design" sections below depend on this.
#
#   2. Ignoring by extension is a blunt instrument. Nearly every build
#      output this project produces already lives under `target/` or
#      `dist/`, which are ignored wholesale. A blanket `*.exe` adds no
#      protection there and actively endangers `vendor/whisper/`, which
#      must stay tracked. Where a stray artifact really might land loose
#      in the repository, the pattern is anchored with a leading `/` so
#      it applies to the root only.
#
# Check what a rule does before trusting it:
#   git check-ignore -v --no-index <path>
#
# List tracked files that a rule would now exclude (should be empty):
#   git ls-files -i -c --exclude-standard
# ============================================================


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Build outputs
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Cargo. This one directory holds every Rust build artifact — objects,
# debug symbols, the MSI and NSIS bundles, nextest reports, coverage
# profiles. Ignoring it makes per-extension rules for those unnecessary.
target/
**/target/
*.rs.bk

# Node and Vite.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dist/
**/dist/
.vite/
**/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Installers or binaries copied to the repository root to test by hand.
# Anchored to `/` so they cannot reach vendor/whisper/.
/*.exe
/*.msi
/*.dll
/*.zip


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Customer data
#
#  This is a call analyzer. Audio, transcripts and exports are the exact
#  material SECURITY.md forbids putting in a public repository, and a
#  dropped sample file is the most plausible way it would happen. Ignored
#  by default; the negations below are the deliberate escape hatch for
#  small, synthetic test fixtures.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.wav
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.aac
*.wma
*.amr
*.gsm
*.au

# Transcript, report and export output written next to the binary.
/transcripts/
/exports/
/reports/
/out/
*.srt
*.vtt

# Deliberate, reviewed test fixtures. Keep these synthetic and short —
# a fixture is not a place to park a real recording.
!**/tests/fixtures/**
!**/testdata/**


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Databases
#
#  ca-store writes to %APPDATA% in normal use, but running the `ca` CLI
#  from the repository root leaves the corpus and its SQLite sidecars here.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.db-journal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Model weights
#
#  ggml-small.en.bin alone is 465 MB. GitHub warns above 50 MB and refuses
#  any single file above 100 MB, so weights are fetched on demand:
#
#      pwsh scripts/fetch-models.ps1
#
#  See docs/CI_CD.md for removing the copy currently still in history.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

models/*.bin
models/*.gguf
models/*.safetensors
models/*.pt
models/*.onnx


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Vendored build inputs — TRACKED BY DESIGN
#
#  vendor/whisper/Release/ is an input, not an output: tauri.conf.json
#  bundles it into the installer, so CI has to receive it from the
#  checkout. Everything else under vendor/ stays out.
#
#  Git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, which
#  is why each level is un-ignored in turn rather than in one pattern.
#  Do not add a blanket *.dll or *.exe rule above this block.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vendor/*
!vendor/whisper/
vendor/whisper/*
!vendor/whisper/Release/
!vendor/whisper/Release/**


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Tauri — TRACKED BY DESIGN
#
#  apps/desktop/src-tauri/gen/schemas/ is generated by tauri-build, and is
#  committed on purpose: the capability schemas are what let an editor
#  validate capabilities/default.json, and CI builds without regenerating
#  them. Nothing here ignores it — this note exists so that a future
#  "ignore generated files" cleanup does not quietly remove it.
#
#  Bundle outputs (.msi, .nsis, .app, .deb) all land under target/ and are
#  covered by the Cargo rule at the top.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  CI and coverage artifacts
#
#  Produced by scripts/preflight.ps1 and by cargo-llvm-cov when either is
#  run from the repository root.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

lcov.info
coverage.txt
coverage/
*.profraw
*.profdata
.nyc_output/
sbom*.json
SHA256SUMS


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Python — scripts/validate_nvidia.py and the tooling around it
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.venv/
venv/


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Editors and IDEs
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

.vscode/
.idea/
.vs/
.fleet/
.zed/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.suo
*.user
.project
.classpath
.settings/

# Claude Code's per-machine overrides. The shared settings.json, if one is
# ever added, is meant to be committed; the .local. variant never is.
.claude/settings.local.json


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  OS junk
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
ehthumbs.db
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Logs, temporary files and leftovers
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.log
*.tmp
*.temp

# Left behind by a failed merge or a patch. The CI repo guard rejects the
# conflict markers themselves; these are the files that accompany them.
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Archives
#
#  Almost always something downloaded or produced rather than authored.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.tgz
*.tar.gz
*.7z
*.rar


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Secrets and credentials — DELIBERATELY LAST
#
#  A later pattern beats an earlier one, so anything below cannot be
#  un-ignored by a negation above it. That matters: the test-fixture
#  exception in the customer-data section is written as
#  `!**/tests/fixtures/**`, which is broad on purpose — a fixture of any
#  type should be committable. Placed earlier, these rules would let a
#  file named `tests/fixtures/.env` or `tests/fixtures/cert.pfx` through.
#  Placed here, nothing can override them.
#
#  Keep this block at the bottom, and add new secret patterns to it rather
#  than anywhere else in the file.
#
#  These are files that must never be committed even once. Removing a key
#  in a later commit does not remove it from history; it means rotating
#  the key.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Code-signing material. The release workflow expects the certificate to
# arrive as a GitHub secret, never as a file — but a developer testing
# `signtool` locally will have one sitting in the working tree.
*.pfx
*.p12
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.jks
*.keystore

# Cloud credentials, for the S3, Azure and GCS sources in ca-ingest.
.aws/
credentials.json
service-account*.json
*_rsa
*_ed25519

# Deliberately no filename heuristic here (*api_key*, *secret* and the
# like). It would silently hide legitimately named source files, and a
# contributor whose file vanished from `git status` has no obvious way to
# work out why. Secrets are caught by their *content* instead: gitleaks
# scans full history in security.yml, and GitHub push protection rejects a
# recognised credential at push time — both configured by
# scripts/setup-branch-protection.ps1.
